					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/augmentedspartan" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1775542); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1775542&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607200937" alt="augmentedspartan" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1775542)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/augmentedspartan" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1775542); return false;" >augmentedspartan</a></span> <span class="odate time_1575225849 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">01 Dec 2019 18:44</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4436177">
									

<p>I understand that the character's behaviour was being affected by the anomaly, but I just couldn't stop thinking about the cliched way characters in horror movies act stupid. It doesn't matter if a character is stupid through bad writing or through anomalous effects, if I they don't act like humans would act, how can I put myself in their shoes?</p>
<p>The behavioural changes weren't enough to make the character's actions interesting or creepy, and just felt like an excuse to have him act stupid. SCP-2678 also had the anomaly affect the character's behaviour, but it was done very creepily and actually felt more than just not caring about stuff. If an anomaly affects someone's personality, it should do more than just feel like a contrived way to make the character not care about the situation.</p>
<p>-1</p>

